  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the maximum magnitude outcome (i.e., the outcome with the largest absolute value) and evaluates which is more favorable, weighted by probability. In this case, the maximum magnitude outcomes are -1000 (50%) for A and -500 (100%) for B. Both are losses, so the more favorable one is -500, and it occurs with higher probability, so the reason would prefer Lottery B. However, the individual chose A and justified it by the chance to avoid any loss (i.e., get 0). This reasoning focuses on the best possible outcome (0) rather than the largest magnitude outcome (-1000). The individual is not considering the largest magnitude outcome at all; in fact, they are avoiding attention to the -1000 loss. Instead, they are focusing on the non-negative outcome. Therefore, the information considered by the individual (presence of a no-loss outcome) differs from that required by the decision reason (largest absolute outcome and its favorability). The justification (choosing to avoid loss) is also inconsistent with the rule (prefer the more favorable high-magnitude outcome weighted by probability), which would favor B. Hence, the individual did not use this decision reason. The reasoning is instead aligned with a 'minimax regret' or 'best outcome' heuristic, not the maximum magnitude rule.",
